1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
America's space technology has taken us to new worlds.

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:11,000
But it's also found a place on Earth.

3
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:15,000
NASA's hand-me-downs save countless lives,

4
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:18,000
conserve our treasure and energy costs,

5
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:21,000
and pack serious power.

6
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:23,000
This is one badass golf cart.

7
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:25,000
They're found in the line of fire,

8
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:27,000
behind your walls,

9
00:00:27,001 --> 00:00:29,000
and even in your bed.

10
00:00:29,001 --> 00:00:33,000
To the moon, beyond, and back again.

11
00:00:33,001 --> 00:00:36,000
It came from outer space.

12
00:00:36,001 --> 00:00:39,000
Now on Modern Marvels.

13
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:58,000
During a marathon, it only takes 30 minutes for a racer's body heat to rise above the century mark.

14
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:06,800
But once he gets past the finish line, it may take only 15 minutes for his body temperature

15
00:01:06,801 --> 00:01:12,760
to plummet below 90 degrees Fahrenheit.

16
00:01:12,761 --> 00:01:22,860
This rapid shift, combined with the slightest chill in the air, can result in hypothermia.

17
00:01:22,861 --> 00:01:27,980
To prevent this potentially lethal condition, runners drape themselves in thermal blankets

18
00:01:27,981 --> 00:01:31,120
made of a special metallic material.

19
00:01:31,121 --> 00:01:36,840
The blanket acts as a radiant barrier.

20
00:01:36,841 --> 00:01:41,320
By reflecting away excess thermal radiation and trapping body heat,

21
00:01:41,321 --> 00:01:47,820
radiant barriers help us maintain a constant temperature.

22
00:01:47,821 --> 00:01:50,940
This innovative blanket has a back story that's literally

23
00:01:50,941 --> 00:01:53,180
out of this world.

24
00:01:53,181 --> 00:01:58,340
Like more than a thousand other cool products, it came from outer space.

25
00:01:58,341 --> 00:02:05,100
From freeze-dried foods to fabric structures, to solar panels, and even sneakers.

26
00:02:05,101 --> 00:02:12,500
Their journey from outer space to your neighborhood is all part of NASA's master plan.

27
00:02:12,501 --> 00:02:20,020
When NASA was created in 1958, Congress mandated that any technology developed for space exploration

28
00:02:20,021 --> 00:02:21,680
would be available for public use.

29
00:02:23,640 --> 00:02:27,360
Well, the objective with NASA was to create a civilian agency

30
00:02:27,361 --> 00:02:29,420
to carry out space and aeronautics research

31
00:02:29,421 --> 00:02:32,120
and its benefits for the nation.

32
00:02:32,600 --> 00:02:34,580
And so being a civilian agency,

33
00:02:34,940 --> 00:02:37,400
the objective was to have open communication

34
00:02:37,401 --> 00:02:40,720
of what our technologies were and make those technologies available.

35
00:02:41,180 --> 00:02:44,020
People often don't understand the connection

36
00:02:44,021 --> 00:02:45,600
that a lot of space technology has,

37
00:02:45,740 --> 00:02:47,780
but there are many, many things that we do every day

38
00:02:47,781 --> 00:02:50,560
that we benefit from as a result of space technology.

39
00:02:52,640 --> 00:02:56,980
Thousands of the heat-saving blankets that resulted from NASA's ongoing policy

40
00:02:56,981 --> 00:03:01,900
have been made here at Star Metallizing in Escondido, California.

41
00:03:04,900 --> 00:03:07,640
The key to making this super-thin form of insulation

42
00:03:07,641 --> 00:03:11,560
is a layer of aluminum only two-hundredths of a micron thick.

43
00:03:13,240 --> 00:03:16,440
That's a thousand times thinner than a sheet of aluminum foil.

44
00:03:17,780 --> 00:03:20,980
and it's applied inside this machine.

45
00:03:21,980 --> 00:03:24,020
What we're looking at is a metalizer,

46
00:03:24,500 --> 00:03:27,640
and what it does is it takes plastic film

47
00:03:27,641 --> 00:03:29,620
and puts a coating of aluminum on it.

48
00:03:30,120 --> 00:03:33,460
The aluminum wire is 99.9% aluminum.

49
00:03:34,240 --> 00:03:36,380
You've got thousands of feet on each spool,

50
00:03:36,880 --> 00:03:40,620
and your metalizer runs all sorts of different kinds of plastic.

51
00:03:42,180 --> 00:03:45,120
The metalizer can aluminum coat plastic goods

52
00:03:45,121 --> 00:03:46,900
of various sizes and thicknesses.

53
00:03:47,780 --> 00:03:52,280
We run basically on this type of stuff about a thousand feet a minute.

54
00:03:52,281 --> 00:03:54,780
Other materials we run a lot slower.

55
00:03:54,781 --> 00:03:57,780
The reason we run slower is optical density.

56
00:03:57,781 --> 00:04:04,780
Some people actually want like a 3.0, meaning they want it so when you hold it up you can't see through it.

57
00:04:04,781 --> 00:04:11,780
In this run, workers are coating 850 pound rolls of polyurethane plastic film

58
00:04:11,781 --> 00:04:17,620
that are 50 mil thick, 60 inches wide, and 70,000 feet long.

59
00:04:21,140 --> 00:04:23,800
Once the roll is loaded and the metallizer is sealed,

60
00:04:24,300 --> 00:04:28,900
the refrigeration unit cools the chamber down to minus 121 degrees Fahrenheit,

61
00:04:29,440 --> 00:04:32,020
which freezes all moisture out of the air.

62
00:04:33,440 --> 00:04:35,480
The mechanical pumps are in the back.

63
00:04:35,900 --> 00:04:37,720
Those actually create a suction.

64
00:04:38,360 --> 00:04:39,780
And there's a blower in front of them,

65
00:04:39,781 --> 00:04:44,780
So you're taking all the air out of this chamber and you're pumping it all the way down.

66
00:04:44,781 --> 00:04:49,780
Now this chamber won't go down to zero atmosphere without that poly-cold.

67
00:04:49,781 --> 00:04:53,780
Because if there's any water, there's trapped oxygen in there.

68
00:04:53,781 --> 00:04:59,780
And without it being frozen, once it's frozen, it doesn't count against it because the oxygen is locked up.

69
00:04:59,781 --> 00:05:04,780
If there's any water in this system at all, it will not pump down.

70
00:05:04,781 --> 00:05:11,920
In the chamber, the heating elements fire up to 600 degrees Fahrenheit, and the reels of plastic film begin to spin.

71
00:05:13,280 --> 00:05:18,140
Each wire then feeds onto its individual heating element, which vaporizes it instantly.

72
00:05:20,380 --> 00:05:28,120
When the aluminum cloud contacts the film, the film turns from clear to silver in an instant, and the metallization is complete.

73
00:05:29,120 --> 00:05:31,540
Did you catch that? Here it is again.

74
00:05:34,780 --> 00:05:43,700
The finished product is a 72 by 48 inch square of life-saving insulation, folded down to

75
00:05:43,701 --> 00:05:48,960
fit in your pocket.

76
00:05:48,961 --> 00:05:54,940
It's certainly come in handy for athletes after a marathon, but the metalization process

77
00:05:54,941 --> 00:06:01,940
was perfected for an entirely different marathon, four decades ago.

78
00:06:01,941 --> 00:06:08,940
space race between the United States and the Soviet Union.

79
00:06:08,941 --> 00:06:12,940
One of the earliest projects out of the gate was America's launch of its first communication

80
00:06:12,941 --> 00:06:17,440
satellite, ECHO-1.

81
00:06:17,441 --> 00:06:25,940
ECHO-1 was an enormous 10-story high balloon satellite made of thick polyester film.

82
00:06:25,941 --> 00:06:31,340
Despite the earthly strength of ECHO's plastic body, NASA engineers knew it wasn't durable

83
00:06:31,341 --> 00:06:38,340
enough to survive the extreme temperature swings of outer space.

84
00:06:38,341 --> 00:06:41,066
So NASA metalized Echo-1's skin.

85
00:06:41,067 --> 00:06:45,580
It acted as a radiant barrier and a means of bouncing

86
00:06:45,581 --> 00:06:49,580
radio signals to and from various locations on Earth.

87
00:06:49,581 --> 00:06:55,000
The radiant barrier dates back to the 1950s and it's been used on every manned and unmanned

88
00:06:55,001 --> 00:07:00,360
NASA mission to date. It's necessary to be light for space travel. It's flexible for

89
00:07:00,361 --> 00:07:04,920
use in, say, an astronaut's space suit, where if conventional insulation had been used,

90
00:07:04,921 --> 00:07:09,960
the astronaut suit would have been seven feet thick.

91
00:07:09,961 --> 00:07:16,760
Another NASA-inspired technology may be right before your very eyes, in the form of advanced

92
00:07:16,761 --> 00:07:21,455
polarized films and UV filters in your sunglasses.

93
00:07:21,456 --> 00:07:25,680
When NASA started working on the Hubble Space

94
00:07:25,681 --> 00:07:27,367
telescope in the 1960s.

95
00:07:27,368 --> 00:07:31,840
Scientists needed a way to control which wavelengths of light

96
00:07:31,841 --> 00:07:35,420
would be recorded by the telescope's cameras.

97
00:07:35,421 --> 00:07:38,840
In order to separate the light from objects

98
00:07:38,841 --> 00:07:45,440
they wanted to observe from other light-emitting bodies, NASA created unique polarizing films

99
00:07:45,441 --> 00:07:49,050
with special light-filtering chemical dyes.

100
00:07:49,051 --> 00:07:52,660
Scientists also incorporated tiny particles

101
00:07:52,661 --> 00:07:59,660
of zinc oxide into the lens to protect the camera from UV radiation.

102
00:08:00,060 --> 00:08:04,200
This level of protection was far above most sunglasses of that time,

103
00:08:04,201 --> 00:08:11,200
which had little if any sun-shielding properties.

104
00:08:11,300 --> 00:08:16,100
A typical green-tinted lens designed to absorb bright yellow and green light

105
00:08:16,101 --> 00:08:21,580
stopped very little of the sun's more harmful rays.

106
00:08:21,581 --> 00:08:27,420
The addition of zinc oxide coatings blocked 99% of harmful blue and ultraviolet light.

107
00:08:28,620 --> 00:08:36,020
And the molecules in the polarized lenses blocked wavelengths of light that matched their arrangement, which greatly reduced glare.

108
00:08:36,021 --> 00:08:47,180
In 1982, sunglasses manufacturer Eagle Eyes became the first to turn NASA's blue and UV

109
00:08:47,181 --> 00:08:51,680
light blockers and polarized lenses into a complete line of shades.

110
00:08:55,180 --> 00:09:02,760
Eagle Eyes produces 250,000 pairs of sunglasses every year, but that's just a fraction of the

111
00:09:02,761 --> 00:09:09,760
60 million pairs of polarized lenses made annually worldwide.

112
00:09:11,440 --> 00:09:18,880
Just as NASA had to protect delicate telescope components from harsh light,

113
00:09:18,881 --> 00:09:26,080
it also needed to protect astronauts from the violent thrust generated during liftoff.

114
00:09:26,081 --> 00:09:30,080
NASA was looking for a soft material

115
00:09:30,081 --> 00:09:34,640
that could help protect the astronauts during lift-off phase.

116
00:09:34,641 --> 00:09:37,280
And if you can imagine the G-force,

117
00:09:37,281 --> 00:09:40,700
or the force that the astronauts feel during takeoff,

118
00:09:40,701 --> 00:09:44,600
is roughly three times, a little more than three times,

119
00:09:44,601 --> 00:09:47,940
the gravitational force that we feel here on Earth.

120
00:09:47,941 --> 00:09:51,320
And then on top of that, the launch vehicle is shaking

121
00:09:51,321 --> 00:09:56,900
and rattling, and so it can be quite uncomfortable.

122
00:09:56,901 --> 00:10:01,900
What was once a crucial safety feature for astronauts is now a luxury for sleepers.

123
00:10:05,900 --> 00:10:09,900
Workers at Tempur-Pedic's 12 and a half acre factory in Duffield, Virginia,

124
00:10:10,900 --> 00:10:14,900
are using NASA technology to help the weary get their 40 winks.

125
00:10:15,900 --> 00:10:23,900
We think that this material is certainly successful at absorbing the tremendous amount of G-force that the astronauts experience.

126
00:10:23,901 --> 00:10:29,080
It is also capable of providing a wonderful night's sleep.

127
00:10:29,360 --> 00:10:32,960
And there's no better way of improving the quality of life than getting a better night's sleep.

128
00:10:33,580 --> 00:10:37,640
The secret pressure-relieving properties of these ultra-comfy mattresses

129
00:10:37,641 --> 00:10:41,640
are the billions of open-structure cells that comprise the material.

130
00:10:42,500 --> 00:10:48,760
Looking at the timber material, you will see billions and billions of uniquely distributed cells

131
00:10:48,761 --> 00:10:50,460
with a lot of half-mirrors.

132
00:10:50,461 --> 00:10:56,460
that simply indicates that you have a uniquely balanced perforation, open cell structures

133
00:10:56,461 --> 00:11:01,000
that gives us the truly pressure-relieving properties of the temper material.

134
00:11:03,200 --> 00:11:06,600
When force is applied to the porous structure of ordinary foam,

135
00:11:07,340 --> 00:11:12,020
most of the energy bounces back because the material resists the force applied to it.

136
00:11:14,260 --> 00:11:19,120
The temper cells absorb shock and disperse energy throughout the material

137
00:11:19,121 --> 00:11:23,900
by releasing air as they conform to the shape of the object that's applying the force.

138
00:11:26,180 --> 00:11:30,820
The Tempur-Pedic mattress-making process is as unique as the material itself.

139
00:11:33,340 --> 00:11:41,800
The first step is to slice a bed-sized sliver from one of these 500-plus pound slabs of Tempur material called a billet.

140
00:11:43,080 --> 00:11:47,760
As you can see, on the slicing machine right here, we're working on a double mattress,

141
00:11:47,761 --> 00:11:51,300
which in size is actually five foot by seven feet.

142
00:11:51,880 --> 00:11:55,880
We'll probably get at least eight to ten plates

143
00:11:55,881 --> 00:11:57,560
out of this billet right here.

144
00:11:57,760 --> 00:11:59,640
It actually slices it, comes over,

145
00:11:59,760 --> 00:12:01,640
transverses it over to the pallet

146
00:12:01,641 --> 00:12:03,420
that you see that it's dropping off right here.

147
00:12:05,360 --> 00:12:07,660
After the machine cuts the entire billet,

148
00:12:08,060 --> 00:12:10,340
it loads the slices onto a giant pallet.

149
00:12:11,200 --> 00:12:14,400
Then a forklift shuttles them over to the lamination station

150
00:12:14,401 --> 00:12:20,400
where other machines will secure them to a separate support material.

151
00:12:20,401 --> 00:12:25,400
And our support material is actually two layers on this one particular mattress that we're doing today.

152
00:12:25,401 --> 00:12:29,400
And actually they start right here. We feed one support layer in the front line.

153
00:12:29,401 --> 00:12:32,400
The other support layer is over here on the other side.

154
00:12:32,401 --> 00:12:37,400
And they both go into the system where they get this special adhesive applied to them.

155
00:12:37,401 --> 00:12:43,400
Next, using more glue, the machine attaches the support layers to two layers of temper material.

156
00:12:43,401 --> 00:12:51,300
Finally, the trimming machine saws off the rough edges.

157
00:12:51,301 --> 00:12:56,120
To ensure that each of its mattresses meets federal standards, Tempur-Pedic's research

158
00:12:56,121 --> 00:13:02,680
and development team puts a random sampling of mattresses to the test.

159
00:13:02,681 --> 00:13:07,940
Tempur-Pedic's infomercials include some not-so-scientific tests of its mattresses,

160
00:13:07,941 --> 00:13:12,560
but they showcase how this NASA technology could save astronauts a lot of backaches.

161
00:13:13,440 --> 00:13:18,320
A very practical approach to see the pressure-relieving properties of a temperate material

162
00:13:18,321 --> 00:13:20,520
is the famous wine glass test.

163
00:13:20,820 --> 00:13:25,920
I will start jumping up and down, and you can see that basically no movement is transferred through the mattress

164
00:13:25,921 --> 00:13:30,520
because of the pressure-relieving properties that the temperate material provides you.

165
00:13:32,800 --> 00:13:36,440
NASA inventions make our day-to-day life not only more comfortable,

166
00:13:36,441 --> 00:13:45,680
but also safer from the battlefield to the operating table.

167
00:13:48,440 --> 00:13:50,960
NASA invented the fruit-flavored drink Tang.

168
00:13:52,120 --> 00:13:52,820
Wrong.

169
00:13:53,320 --> 00:13:57,280
General Foods did, almost five years before John Glenn took his first sip

170
00:13:57,281 --> 00:13:59,540
aboard Friendship 7 in 1962.

171
00:14:00,840 --> 00:14:04,600
It Came From Outer Space will return on Modern Marvels.

172
00:14:06,440 --> 00:14:15,520
In the war-torn countries of Iraq and Afghanistan,

173
00:14:19,060 --> 00:14:22,980
improvised explosive devices have killed more than 2,000 U.S. troops.

174
00:14:25,040 --> 00:14:27,660
But soldiers have gained a friend on the front line

175
00:14:27,661 --> 00:14:31,980
that's equipped to venture into harm's way as their eyes, ears, and voice.

176
00:14:33,240 --> 00:14:35,100
One that always follows orders.

177
00:14:36,440 --> 00:14:39,440
has no fear and feels no pain.

178
00:14:40,440 --> 00:14:44,440
PAKBOT, a creation of iRobot in Burlington, Massachusetts,

179
00:14:44,441 --> 00:14:49,440
was deployed to Afghanistan for its first mission in July 2002.

180
00:14:52,440 --> 00:14:55,440
It's a remote-controlled reconnaissance robot

181
00:14:55,441 --> 00:15:00,440
that uses its cameras and sensors to gather information about enemy territory

182
00:15:00,441 --> 00:15:03,440
before the soldiers controlling it move in.

183
00:15:06,440 --> 00:15:10,560
This cyber warrior has become a valued ally of the troops it helps protect.

184
00:15:11,640 --> 00:15:16,660
There's one example where one of our robots was blown up,

185
00:15:17,300 --> 00:15:23,800
and the EOD technician responsible for the robot brought it back to the repair depot with tears in his eyes,

186
00:15:24,360 --> 00:15:27,540
almost like it was a fallen comrade saying, can you fix this?

187
00:15:27,980 --> 00:15:31,680
You know, they'd marked every time it successfully defeated an IED,

188
00:15:32,320 --> 00:15:35,760
and, you know, this person was diswrought, and rightfully so.

189
00:15:35,761 --> 00:15:38,240
this robot may have saved his life.

190
00:15:39,040 --> 00:15:40,580
And that's very powerful stuff.

191
00:15:41,900 --> 00:15:45,660
PacBot can trace its origins and its ability to tough it out in a battle zone

192
00:15:45,661 --> 00:15:49,000
to NASA's discovery program in 1996.

193
00:15:52,580 --> 00:15:57,600
That project created NASA's first Mars rover, Sojourner.

194
00:16:01,160 --> 00:16:04,780
One of the main challenges confronting NASA engineers on the project

195
00:16:04,781 --> 00:16:09,520
was how to protect the rover's delicate electronics from the impact of landing on Mars,

196
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:14,000
as well as from the harsh Martian environment itself.

197
00:16:16,240 --> 00:16:22,660
They solved the problem by housing the robot's complex electronics within a strong sheet metal chassis.

198
00:16:23,940 --> 00:16:32,860
The manufacturing techniques for very tightly integrating mechanics and sensors with the mechanical design,

199
00:16:32,861 --> 00:16:35,800
and it's sealed against the elements.

200
00:16:36,180 --> 00:16:37,440
There's no exposed wires.

201
00:16:37,620 --> 00:16:42,140
All of these are design criteria that existed at NASA,

202
00:16:42,380 --> 00:16:46,000
and as we brought this philosophy over to I-Robot,

203
00:16:46,500 --> 00:16:48,740
enabled the existence of the Pac-Bot.

204
00:16:50,740 --> 00:16:54,080
Pac-Bot has one key advantage over the rovers on Mars.

205
00:16:54,980 --> 00:16:58,380
Operators can alter its capabilities with a variety of add-ons.

206
00:16:59,500 --> 00:17:02,140
This payload is mounted on with six bolts.

207
00:17:02,141 --> 00:17:05,140
You take that off and you can add another payload.

208
00:17:05,141 --> 00:17:09,900
You can add chemical biological sensors on it, and you can add self-navigation sensors

209
00:17:09,901 --> 00:17:10,900
on it.

210
00:17:10,901 --> 00:17:15,300
So the system is very modular and can be adapted for many different purposes.

211
00:17:15,301 --> 00:17:21,200
This is a system that is designed to respond to snipers or mortar fire or any kind of loud

212
00:17:21,201 --> 00:17:22,200
noise.

213
00:17:22,201 --> 00:17:27,380
And the robot will automatically turn, look and listen at whatever made that sound.

214
00:17:32,140 --> 00:17:39,880
What you just heard was a simulated gunshot, and that made the iRobot Packbot with the Red Owl Kit respond.

215
00:17:39,881 --> 00:17:45,460
It provides a series of sensors here in the payload that give information back to the soldier.

216
00:17:45,960 --> 00:17:52,880
For instance, it has a zoom camera, a thermal camera right here, laser illumination, and laser range finding.

217
00:17:53,080 --> 00:17:57,620
So it can look at that target and also provide a range distance back to that target.

218
00:17:57,621 --> 00:18:04,480
The rugged design features that help the Mars rovers negotiate hostile Martian terrain

219
00:18:04,481 --> 00:18:08,980
have equipped PacBot to succeed in the harsh landscape of war.

220
00:18:10,980 --> 00:18:17,440
Our goal when we started the company was to create robots that could touch people's lives on a daily basis.

221
00:18:18,160 --> 00:18:23,560
To see a postcard coming back from Iraq from a soldier who said,

222
00:18:23,561 --> 00:18:25,788
you saved the life today.

223
00:18:25,789 --> 00:18:31,580
I mean, oh my God, that's so validating and so wonderful to think

224
00:18:31,581 --> 00:18:36,920
that we've made that kind of difference. There's another robot, one developed for

225
00:18:36,921 --> 00:18:41,520
NASA's International Space Station, that's also lending Earthlings a helping hand.

226
00:18:43,920 --> 00:18:50,960
It's actually the Barrett hand, and it's attached to the whole arm manipulation system, or WHAM.

227
00:18:53,560 --> 00:18:56,340
This dexterous wonder got its start in 1988,

228
00:18:57,080 --> 00:19:01,760
as NASA began the massive undertaking of building its part of the International Space Station.

229
00:19:04,620 --> 00:19:09,640
NASA wanted a robotic arm that could retrieve tools and move building materials

230
00:19:09,641 --> 00:19:12,000
for astronauts working outside the ISS.

231
00:19:14,160 --> 00:19:17,660
There was a solicitation that came out of the government

